{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>YHWH shall reign forever and ever</b> — one says that He has now shown that He is King and Sovereign over all, for He saved His servants and destroyed those who rebelled against Him; so may it be His will to do so in all generations forever — He will not remove His eyes from the righteous, nor conceal Himself from the wicked who do evil. And many verses come like this, such as \"YHWH shall reign forever, your Elohim, O Zion, for all generations, Hallelujah\" (Psalms 146:10), \"May the name of YHWH be blessed from now and forevermore\" (Psalms 113:2), \"And YHWH shall be King over all the earth\" (Zechariah 14:9). And Onkelos (Onkelos on Exodus 15:18) was apprehensive about it, because the kingship belongs to Elohim for all eternity, and therefore he rendered it in the participle tense: \"The kingship of YHWH stands firm forever and for all eternities,\" in the manner of \"Your kingship is a kingship of all worlds\" (Psalms 145:13). But I have not understood his reasoning in this, for it is written \"May the glory of YHWH be forever\" (Psalms 104:31), \"May His glory fill all the earth\" (Psalms 72:19) — may He be magnified and sanctified — \"May Your name be faithful and magnified\" (I Chronicles 17:24). And it is possible that their reason is according to the secret of the blessings:"], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "The phrase 'who do evil' renders המריעים, literally 'who do harm/evil'; the text appears to continue beyond the given excerpt at 'according to the secret of the blessings,' which suggests a kabbalistic explanation follows but is cut off."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:08:43.670928776Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}